The distinction is decisive: $\begin{aligned}
  R_{\mu\nu}&=0,
  & R&=0
  &&\text{for }r>0, \notag\\
  R_{\alpha\beta\gamma\delta}
  R^{\alpha\beta\gamma\delta}
  &=\frac{48G^2M^2}{r^6}.
  \end{aligned}$ The Kretschmann scalar is finite at $r=2GM$ and diverges at $r=0$. The horizon singularity is a coordinate failure; the central singularity is curvature.

