p.src:
The radial tidal scale is set by $\left|R_{\hat 0\hat r\hat 0\hat r}\right|
  \sim\frac{GM}{r^3}.$ At the horizon this scales as $\frac{GM}{r_{\mathrm s}^3}
  =\frac{1}{8G^2M^2}.$ Thus a stellar-mass black hole can produce destructive horizon-scale tidal forces, whereas the horizon of a supermassive black hole can be locally gentle. In either case the tidal field diverges as $r\to0$.

