p.src:
For a body of radius $R$ and mean density $\bar\rho$, $M=\frac{4\pi}{3}\bar\rho R^3,
  \qquad
  R=\left(\frac{3M}{4\pi\bar\rho}\right)^{1/3}.$ At fixed $\bar\rho$, the material radius grows as $M^{1/3}$, while the Schwarzschild radius grows as $M$. No matter how small the density is, a sufficiently large mass satisfies $R<2GM.$ At the threshold $R=2GM$, the corresponding mean density is $\bar\rho_{\mathrm{BH}}
  =\frac{3M}{4\pi(2GM)^3}
  =\frac{3}{32\pi G^3M^2}.$ The larger the black hole, the smaller its horizon-scale mean density.

